  "text": "A chemically treated stalk from a common freshwater ciliate stays relaxed when the surrounding solution is fairly acidic (around pH 6.8 or lower). Raising the acidity level just a little to about pH 7.0 makes the stalk shorten dramatically. The same strong shortening also occurs at pH 6.8 if the concentration of calcium ions is raised to a very high level. The extent of relaxation at lower acidity is helped by the cell’s energy‑carrying molecule together with magnesium ions, and these three factors together decide how much the stalk will contract at pH 7.0. Calcium ions mainly act by lowering the acidity threshold needed for contraction, shifting it from about pH 7.5 down to the stable pH 6.8 when calcium is absent. The greatest shortening caused by the energy molecule is seen only at pH 7.0 when both magnesium and calcium ions are present at the specified concentrations. It is proposed that calcium, magnesium and the energy molecule bind to the contractile proteins, forming a complex that is relaxed at pH 6.8 and becomes shortened when the acidity rises slightly or when calcium is added."
